      {
        "name": "label?",
        "type": "string",
        "notes": "The accessible name of the control *and* of the `search` landmark. Never defaulted to a string: a library that ships \"Search\" ships it in English.",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:47"
      },
      {
        "name": "clearLabel?",
        "type": "string",
        "notes": "The accessible name of the clear button. Defaults to `\"Clear search\"` — the one string this component ships, because the alternative is an icon-only button with no name at all (see the machine for why this and `label` differ).",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:53"
      },
      {
        "name": "cancelLabel?",
        "type": "string",
        "notes": "The accessible name of the cancel button. Defaults to `\"Cancel search\"`, for the same reason `clearLabel` is defaulted — and the reason is stronger here: the clear button is out of the tab order, this one is a tab stop a keyboard user meets head-on.",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:60"
      },
      {
        "name": "clearOnEscape?",
        "type": "boolean",
        "notes": "Escape empties the field. On by default here — that is the whole difference between this presentation and a text field — and it only ever consumes the key when there is something to clear.",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:66"
      },
      {
        "name": "hotkey?",
        "type": "SearchHotkeyPredicate",
        "notes": "Opt-in activation shortcut, read once when the effect installs: pass it at mount. Absent means no document listener at all, which is why a page that did not ask keeps the browser's own find-in-page intact.",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:72"
      },
      {
        "name": "onValueChange?",
        "type": "((details: SearchFieldValueChangeDetails) => void)",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:73"
      },
      {
        "name": "onCancel?",
        "type": "(() => void)",
        "notes": "The cancel gesture happened. It is an **addition** to `onValueChange` and never a replacement — the value went to the empty string, and a consumer filtering on the value has to see that or their list stays filtered — and it is fired **last**, after the focus has gone back to the control. That order is the contract: `onCancel` is the last word, so a consumer who moves the focus inside it (closing a search popover, say) keeps it. `api.cancel()` on an already-empty field fires this and *not* `onValueChange`: cancelling is an intention, not a change of value.",
        "source": "search-field.types.ts:85"
      }
    ]
  },
